.SS Abstract
SETJMP provides a unified interface for non\-local control flow using
the standard C \f[CR]setjmp\f[R] and \f[CR]longjmp\f[R] mechanisms.
.PP
It allows an execution environment to be saved and later restored,
making it possible to return to a previously established point in the
call stack without propagating normal function returns through every
intermediate function.
.PP
Typical use cases include:
.IP \(bu 2
Recovering from deeply nested error conditions.
.IP \(bu 2
Implementing lightweight exception\-like control flow in C.
.IP \(bu 2
Returning to a known execution point after an error or exceptional
condition.
.IP \(bu 2
Supporting control\-flow mechanisms in environments where a conventional
exception system is unavailable.
.PP
SETJMP maps the CMT uppercase interface to the native
\f[CR]setjmp.h\f[R] implementation when a compatible implementation is
available.
.PP
The interface is intentionally kept small:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]JMP_BUF\f[R] \- jump\-buffer type.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]SETJMP\f[R] \- saves an execution environment.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]LONGJMP\f[R] \- restores a previously saved environment.
.PP
On platforms where a native \f[CR]setjmp.h\f[R] implementation cannot be
detected, a fallback implementation may be provided in the future.
